      "status": "ok",
      "output": {
        "decision": "answer",
        "reason": "The evidence identifies the two seq2seq shortcomings (overconfidence in predictions and incomplete transcripts when language models are used) and describes the proposed solutions in detail: label smoothing to address overconfidence/over-sharp predictions, and three decoding strategies for incomplete transcripts—forbidding EOS emission unless its probability is close to the most probable token, a length-promoting beam search criterion, and a coverage penalty term that counts frames with cumulative attention above a threshold (recomputed at each beam search step)."
      },
      "elapsed_ms": 6931.0289999702945
    },
    {
      "stage": "Generate",
      "attempt": 1,
      "input": {
        "decision": "answer"
      },
      "status": "ok",
      "citation_validation": {
        "id_valid": true,
        "source_consistent": true,
        "semantic_support": "not_evaluated"
      },
      "output": {
        "claims": [
          {
            "key": "two_shortcomings",
            "text": "The paper identifies two seq2seq shortcomings: overconfidence in its predictions and a tendency to produce incomplete transcriptions when language models are used.",
            "citations": [
              {
                "paper_id": "1612.02695",
                "chunk_id": "c-c1a01afddd733ef65ed6d199"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "key": "eos_probability_strategy",
            "text": "The first strategy does not change the beam search criterion but forbids emitting the EOS token unless its probability is within a set range of that of the most probable token; it prevents truncations but is ineffective against omissions in the middle of the transcript.",
            "citations": [
              {
                "paper_id": "1612.02695",
                "chunk_id": "c-753acdce1e13381d0f0064dc"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "key": "coverage_term",
            "text": "To prevent looping, the authors propose a coverage term that counts the number of frames whose cumulative attention exceeds a threshold, recomputed at each beam search iteration using all attention weights produced up to that step.",
            "citations": [
              {
                "paper_id": "1612.02695",
                "chunk_id": "c-753acdce1e13381d0f0064dc"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "key": "coverage_at_decode_time",
            "text": "Their solution employs a coverage penalty at decode time only, which is described as most similar to the one used by the Google Translation system.",
            "citations": [
              {
                "paper_id": "1612.02695",
                "chunk_id": "c-7549a11343a20fbc64d6dc01"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "key": "label_smoothing_regularizer",
            "text": "Label smoothing is discussed as a regularization technique that increases the entropy of a model's predictions and prevents saturating the SoftMax nonlinearity, yielding better gradient flow to lower layers.",
            "citations": [
              {
                "paper_id": "1612.02695",
                "chunk_id": "c-7549a11343a20fbc64d6dc01"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "key": "global_vs_local_normalization",
            "text": "The paper notes that global normalization is expensive because each training step requires running beam search inference, and it remains to be established whether globally normalized models can be approximated by cheaper locally normalized models with proper regularization such as label smoothing.",
            "citations": [
              {
                "paper_id": "1612.02695",
                "chunk_id": "c-7549a11343a20fbc64d6dc01"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "key": "results_wer",
            "text": "With the proposed solutions, the system achieves 10.6% WER on Wall Street Journal without separate language models, and 6.7% WER together with a trigram language model.",
            "citations": [
              {
                "paper_id": "1612.02695",
                "chunk_id": "c-c1a01afddd733ef65ed6d199"
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "decision": "answer"
